 { Black has just played ...Rf8-e8, saving himself from further material losses. White reacted very opportunely, bearing in mind the idea of the magic distance. } 1. { -KEY- } Rxe8+ ( { After } 1. Ra1 Bb7 2. f3 { White has good winning chances due to his extra pawn, but Boleslavsky's approach is more technical. } ) 
Nxe8 2. Be5 $1 { A key move, which leaves the knight on e8 restricted for the rest of the game. We saw an almost identical case in Chapter Two (Example 126, Fischer-Addison). } ( { In contrast, after } 2. gxf3 $6 Nxd6 { White would suffer from a bad structure. } ) 
Bc6 3. b4 $1 { Fixing a weakness on a6. } h5 ( { Evidently, } 3... Nf6 { at any moment will be met by } 4. Bxf6 { and the knight vs. bishop ending arising after } gxf6 5. f3 { is easily won thanks to the extra pawn - Bronstein. } ) 
4. f3 { The rest is relatively easy, as Black is virtually a piece down. } Kh7 5. Ne2 g5 6. Kf2 h4 7. g3 hxg3+ 8. hxg3 Kg6 9. g4 $1 { A move with several virtues: the enemy king is restricted, further pawn exchanges are avoided, and a future target is created on g5. } Bb7 10. Ke3 Bc6 11. Nc3 Bb7 12. Ne4 Bd5 ( { Or } 12... Bc8 13. Nc5 { and it is actually the knight who is dominating the bishop, with a decisive Kd4-d5 next. } ) 
13. Nc5 Kf7 14. Nxa6 Ke6 15. Bc3 Ba8 16. Nc5+ Kf7 17. Ne4 Kg6 18. Be5 Bd5 19. Nd2 Kf7 ( { Black resigned at the same time, in view of } 19... Kf7 20. Kd4 Ke6 21. Ne4 { It is a striking fact that the knight never left e8. } ) 

