 { When the king fights against a knight, a good location tends to be at the distance of two squares diagonally from it - not so much for restrictive means, as to avoid future checks. Here is a simple example to demonstrate this point.In order to win White must prevent the knight from reaching the b-file. } 1. { -KEY- } b6 Nf4+ ( { a) After both } 1... Ne7 2. Kc5 $1 ) 
 ( { b) And } 1... Nf8 2. Kd6 $1 { the white king dominates the knight. } ( { These are the only winning moves - in this line, for example, } 1... Nf4+ 1. Kc6 $2 { would only draw after } Ne6 ) 
 ) 
2. Kd6 $1 { Applying the magic distance. } ( { a) Both } 2. Kc6 $2 Ne6 ) 
 ( { b) And } 2. Ke5 $2 Nd3 { followed by ...Nb4-a6, would allow Black to draw. } ) 
 ( { c) As for } 2. Kc4 $6 { after } Ng6 $1 { White would have to return to the main line with } 3. Kd5 ( { since } 3. b7 $2 { fails to } Ne5 4. Kd5 Nd7 ) 
Nf4 4. Kd6 $1 { in order to win. } ) 
h4 3. b7 { White wins. } 
 *

