 { With a rook fighting against a knight, the same distance of two squares diagonally remains highly relevant, as in this simple example. The king and rook vs. king and knight ending is only rarely won, but here the location of Black's king proves fatal. } 1. { -KEY- } Rd5 $1 { The magic distance in order to limit the knight. Incidentally, changing subject for a moment, two fellow pieces located at the magic distance from each other (e.g. Kf3 and Qd1) can't be forked by an enemy knight. } ( { A good alternative is } 1. Re6 { , e.g. } Nh8 2. Kf6 Nf7 3. Ra6 Nh6 4. Kg6 { with a decisive double threat, as observed by Stigma. } ) 
Nh8+ ( { Obviously, } 1... Kf8 { runs into } 2. Rf5 ) 
2. Kf6 Nf7 3. Rd7 $1 { Compared to the initial position White's king is now on f6, which means there is no fork on e5. } ( { Another winning option is } 3. Rd4 $5 Nh6 4. Rc4 $1 { (zugzwang) } Kh7 5. Rh4 ) 
Nh8 ( { Or } 3... Nh6 4. Kg6 $1 { with a decisive double threat. } ) 
4. Ra7 $1 { Losing a tempo. } Kf8 5. Ra8# 
