 { By the way, the magic distance isn't an exclusive endgame subject. Here are two positions from earlier stages of the game, appearing in 'Mastering Chess Strategy' and 'Mastering Opening Strategy', respectively. } 1. { -KEY- } e4 ( { J.Hellsten-A.Brkljaca, Belgrade 2002 (analysis variation): } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 O-O 5. Nge2 d5 6. a3 Be7 7. Nf4 c6 8. Bd3 dxc4 9. Bxc4 Nbd7 10. O-O e5 11. Nfe2 Nb6 12. Ba2 exd4 13. Nxd4 c5 14. Nf3 Bg4 15. e4 Qxd1 16. Rxd1 Bxf3 17. gxf3 { Black would really like to seize the d4-square with one of his knights, but that will take him considerable time (if it is even possible), since both knights are on the magic distance from d4. Probably he will have to adjust his plans - fight for the open file by } Rfd8 { for instance, when White keeps a pleasant advantage after } 18. Be3 { thanks to his bishop pair and mobile pawn centre. } ) 
c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 e6 4. O-O Nge7 5. Re1 a6 6. Bf1 d5 7. exd5 Qxd5 8. Bd3 Ng6 9. Be4 Qd6 10. c3 Be7 11. d4 O-O 12. Be3 cxd4 13. cxd4 Bd7 14. Nc3 Rad8 15. Rc1 { A bishop on e4 is not a typical feature in such IQP structures, but here this idea works very well since both black knights are located at the 'magic' distance from e4, which means that neither will be able to challenge the bishop in the near future. } ( { The game continued } 15. Rc1 f5 16. Bc2 f4 $6 17. Ne4 Qd5 18. Bd2 $1 { preparing Bb3, with a clear advantage for White. } ) 

